Two changes to wtransport 0.7.2's server accept, applied by the release build
to the vendored crate; development runs unpatched.

1. Accept at 0.5-RTT. A quinn server connection may send as soon as its
   handshake flight is out, but wtransport waited for the whole handshake
   before its driver sent the HTTP/3 SETTINGS, and Chromium sends the
   CONNECT only once SETTINGS arrive: a cold session cost three round trips.
   With SETTINGS at 0.5-RTT it costs two (Chromium at a 47 ms RTT: `ready`
   145 ms -> 97 ms). The connection is taken only once the whole ClientHello
   is in: taken earlier, with the client's stream limits still unknown, the
   driver's control-stream open waited on a credit that woke nobody, and
   Chromium never got SETTINGS (seen behind a 20 Mbit/s link, where the
   ClientHello's two Initial packets arrive apart).
2. Close an abandoned connection. The driver's worker holds its own clone
   of the connection, so when the gate gave up on a handshake (its deadline)
   the connection stayed open, kept alive by keep-alives, and the browser
   waited for an answer that never came. The connection now closes unless
   a session request comes out of the future.

--- a/src/endpoint.rs
+++ b/src/endpoint.rs
@@ -613,8 +613,37 @@
 
     fn new(quic_incoming: quinn::Incoming) -> Self {
         Self(Box::pin(async move {
-            let quic_connection = quic_incoming.await?;
-            Self::accept(quic_connection).await
+            // Server side, into_0rtt always succeeds: the connection is usable
+            // for 0.5-RTT data, so SETTINGS leave with the server's handshake
+            // flight instead of a round trip later.
+            // Only once the whole ClientHello is in: before that the
+            // client's transport parameters (its stream limits) are unknown,
+            // the driver's open of its control stream waits on a credit whose
+            // arrival wakes nobody, and SETTINGS never leave. A ClientHello
+            // split across two Initial packets that arrive apart did that.
+            let mut connecting = quic_incoming.accept()?;
+            let _ = connecting.handshake_data().await;
+            let quic_connection = match connecting.into_0rtt() {
+                Ok((connection, _)) => connection,
+                Err(connecting) => connecting.await?,
+            };
+            // The driver's worker holds a clone of the connection, so a
+            // caller that gives up on this future (a handshake deadline)
+            // would leave the connection open, kept alive by keep-alives,
+            // with the client waiting for an answer forever. Close it
+            // unless a session request comes out.
+            struct CloseOnDrop(quinn::Connection, bool);
+            impl Drop for CloseOnDrop {
+                fn drop(&mut self) {
+                    if !self.1 {
+                        self.0.close(0u32.into(), b"abandoned");
+                    }
+                }
+            }
+            let mut guard = CloseOnDrop(quic_connection.clone(), false);
+            let request = Self::accept(quic_connection).await;
+            guard.1 = request.is_ok();
+            request
         }))
     }
 
